---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import { FileText } from '@lucide/astro';
---

<Layout title="About - Saad Shahd">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-20">
    <h1 class="text-5xl font-bold mb-12">About</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-8 leading-relaxed">
        I build systems for a reason—I chase the 'why' without fixating on the 'how.' Over 15 years, I developed a philosophy that makes the 'how' obvious.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        I'm a Senior Software Engineer at Wise, doing principal-level work on product discovery. Currently leading the editorial-platform project: a comprehensive content system enabling 20 teams to collaborate on wise.com without friction, inconsistency, or incorrectness. The challenge isn't just technical—it's solving global→local fintech at scale: regulations differ by market, offerings vary by country, and cultural nuances matter.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        My approach is rooted in functional programming principles I learned the hard way—through production bugs in high-velocity startup environments at Instabug, StatsBomb, and Wise. Traditional thinking says you trade velocity for technical debt. I learned that functional thinking eliminates that trade-off entirely. When you separate behavior from state and make illegal states unrepresentable, you move fast <em>and</em> stay confident.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        At StatsBomb, I designed a realtime collection system that reduced 90-minute match collection from 16 man-hours to 4 concurrent collectors. I built a dataspec and DSL that let PMs define sport collection rules without engineering involvement. Why did it scale? The architecture separated collection rules from execution logic. New sports became configuration, not code. We never had to choose between velocity and correctness.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        I don't predict the future—the future is ambiguous. The best we can do is embrace that ambiguity and design lean systems that adapt without breaking.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-8 leading-relaxed">
        I'm exploring how LLMs change what's possible in software—not as automation, but as cognitive partners for design. I work primarily in TypeScript, Python, Go, and Clojure.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg font-medium text-gray-800">
        I optimize for the "why" first. The right question makes implementation obvious.
      </p>

      <!-- Resume CTA -->
      <div class="mt-10 mb-12">
        <Button href="/resume" variant="primary" size="md" class="gap-2">
          <FileText size={20} />
          View Full Resume
        </Button>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-12 mb-6">Technical Expertise</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
        <li>Functional programming and separation of concerns</li>
        <li>Distributed systems correctness</li>
        <li>Real-time event processing and collection systems</li>
        <li>Content architecture and editorial platforms</li>
        <li>Global→local scaling (fintech regulations, i18n/l10n)</li>
        <li>Developer experience and platform thinking</li>
        <li>Team leadership and principal-level architectural decisions</li>
        <li>LLM-augmented development patterns</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-12 mb-6">Career Highlights</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-700">
        <li><strong>Editorial Platform at Wise:</strong> Leading comprehensive content platform for 20 teams collaborating on wise.com. The challenge: global→local fintech content at scale. The solution: consistent design patterns with maintained developer experience. Complex regulations become structured data.</li>
        <li><strong>Real-Time Collection System at StatsBomb:</strong> Cut 90-minute match collection from 16 man-hours to 4 concurrent collectors. Built dataspec + DSL empowering PMs to define sport collection rules without engineering. Configuration over code enabled rapid expansion.</li>
        <li><strong>Startup to Enterprise Journey:</strong> Learned functional architecture through production reality. High-velocity environments at Instabug, StatsBomb, and Wise taught me: correctness and speed aren't opposed—they're complementary.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
